  "preview": "A roll of tape has a known outer diameter, inner diameter, and tape thickness. How long is the tape when fully unrolled? (A modeling task.)",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Apply circle measurements (circumference, area) to model a real-world unrolling problem, deciding what assumptions are reasonable and what information matters.",
    "studentPrompt": "You have a roll of masking tape.\n\n- Outer diameter of the roll: 12 cm\n- Inner diameter (cardboard core): 6 cm\n- Thickness of the tape itself: 0.1 mm\n\nIf you fully unroll the tape, how long will it be?\n\nFirst: estimate. Write down a guess. Then: figure it out.",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/2887719/pexels-photo-2887719.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "A roll of green tape stands out against a coral background in a minimalist setting.",
      "credit": "Photo by Gilmer Diaz Estela on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Hold up a roll of tape. Ask: 'How long is the tape if we unrolled it?' Take guesses (low, high, your best). Write the guesses on the board. Don't tell them what information they need — let them ask.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: an actual roll of tape",
      "Calculator (allowed for grade 7+)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "circumference",
      "circles",
      "area",
      "estimation",
      "modeling",
      "annulus"
    ],
    "taskType": "three-act",
    "btcFit": "Strong three-act / modeling task. Low floor (anyone can guess), high ceiling (the area-based solution requires algebra). Real materials (a roll of tape) raise engagement.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "**Approach 1 (additive — simpler, less precise):**\nThink of the tape as a sequence of concentric circles. The radius starts at 3 cm (inner) and grows to 6 cm (outer). Each layer of tape adds 0.01 cm (0.1 mm) to the radius. Number of layers = 3 cm / 0.01 cm = 300. Average circumference = π × (3 + 6) ≈ 28.3 cm. Total tape length ≈ 300 × 28.3 ≈ 8,490 cm ≈ 85 m.\n\n**Approach 2 (area-based — more elegant):**\nThe cross-section of the rolled tape is an annulus (ring) with area π(6² - 3²) = 27π ≈ 84.8 cm². When unrolled, the tape is a long thin rectangle. Its area is the same: thickness × length. Length = 84.8 cm² / 0.01 cm ≈ 8,480 cm ≈ 85 m.\n\nBoth give roughly the same answer (~85 m).",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating the tape as a single circle and using just the outer circumference (= 2π × 6 ≈ 37.7 cm). This vastly underestimates.\n- Forgetting to subtract the inner core area in the annulus method.\n- Mixing units (mm vs cm). Make units explicit on the board.\n- Using diameter when they should use radius (or vice versa) in the formulas.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups got different answers. What assumptions are they each making?\n- The additive approach uses an 'average circumference.' Why does that work?\n- The area approach uses cross-sectional area. Why is that the same as the unrolled rectangle's area?\n- If the tape were thicker, would it be longer or shorter? What if the inner core were larger?",
    "extensions": "- A toilet paper roll is 4 inches in outer diameter, 1.5 inches inner diameter, with paper thickness 0.005 in. How many feet of paper?\n- A rolled-up garden hose has outer radius 30 cm, inner radius 10 cm, hose diameter 1 cm. How long is the hose? (Note: now you're stacking circles, not winding flat tape.)\n- Work backwards: a tape claims to be 50 m long, with thickness 0.1 mm and inner diameter 6 cm. What's the outer diameter?",
    "sampleSolutions": "Both approaches give ~85 m. The area-based approach is cleaner: cross-section area of annulus = π(R² - r²) = π(36 - 9) = 27π ≈ 84.8 cm². Unrolled tape is a rectangle of thickness 0.01 cm and length L, with area 0.01 × L = 84.8, so L = 8,480 cm ≈ 84.8 m.",
